)), but this means that if you remove this component of income related to staking activities you may need to increase the "default" income (i.e. issuance) in order to make up for this lost income affecting staker margins to a point where they become less sustainable.

e.g. in the solana issuance reduction proposal, the logic was that "we can afford to drastically cut issuance because validators make so much money from MEV". It's a bad argument because you don't really want your validators to be business-viable just because of the money they make from MEV (in general MEV is bad — we want to minimize it and to push as much of it back to the source as possible) — it creates perverse incentives and it also means that once you "fix" (or meaningfully reduce/compress) MEV then you need to worry about adjusting issuance again.

So in general MEV Burn good, but I disagree that it releases pressure on these debates, it will actually accentuate the importance of finding  the right level of issuance.

